00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,015
Slide 3 illustrates GUEST VS.

2
00:00:03,015 --> 00:00:10,340
LANDLORD: The Desktop Schism. James, explain this landlord analogy to our students.

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:20,340
Think of Microsoft as the building landlord: they own the foundation, the walls, the electricity, and the front door lock—which is the Windows kernel and Win32 APIs!

4
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:23,931
And Google is the tenant living in the building!

5
00:00:23,931 --> 00:00:29,383
For 15 years, Google was content just staying inside the Chrome browser apartment.

6
00:00:29,383 --> 00:00:34,970
But now, Microsoft put Copilot directly on the taskbar and on the physical keyboard!

7
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:36,703
So Google panicked!

8
00:00:36,703 --> 00:00:41,824
If users ask Copilot on their desktop, they stop searching on google.com!

9
00:00:41,824 --> 00:00:48,350
So Google created the Google App for Windows—a desktop search bar that hijacks `Alt + Space`!

10
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:57,140
But look at the technical cost on the right card: because Google is a guest, they can't use native Windows UI controls easily.

11
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:03,800
They had to pack an entire WebView2 Chromium browser into that tiny search bar, eating 1.2GB of RAM!

12
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:11,089
Imagine renting a room and bringing in a massive 2-ton diesel generator just to turn on a single light bulb!

13
00:01:11,089 --> 00:01:12,620
That is what Google did!

14
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:16,656
Platform friction forces architectural bloat.

15
00:01:16,656 --> 00:01:20,940
Let us examine the Trojan Horse mechanics on Slide 4.

